---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Research — Paulina Cywoniuk"
  description="Master's thesis on UI marker design and narrative immersion in VR. Findings, design implications, and downloadable papers."
>
 <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="research-hero">
    <p class="research-eyebrow">Master's Thesis · 2026</p>
    <h1 class="research-title">
      How to mark <em>interactable objects</em> in a VR experience without breaking players' <em>immersion</em> into the game story.
    </h1>
    <p class="research-lede">
      A master's <span class="hl">UI/UX research</span> of interaction markers design
      in VR narration-based games — quantitative and qualitative
      between-subjects experiment with 35 participants across 3 conditions.
    </p>
    <div class="research-meta">
      <span><strong>Breda University of Applied Sciences</strong></span>
      <span>·</span>
      <span>MSc Game Technology</span>
      <span>·</span>
      <span>2026</span>
    </div>
  </section>

 <!-- ABSTRACT BLOCK -->
  <section class="research-section abstract-block">
    <h2>Abstract</h2>
    <p>
      This paper investigates the influence of <strong>interaction marker design</strong>
      in VR experiences on players' ability to become immersed in the narration of the game.
      Following diegetics, semiotics, and affordance theories, the researcher created a VR
      game <em>"Echoes of Abandonment"</em> with markers of interactable objects present
      in the digital room, used in the experiment.
    </p>
    <p>
      The methodology consisted of participants playing the VR game, then filling out a
      questionnaire with the <em>Narrative Engagement Scale</em> (Busselle and Bilandzic, 2009)
      and <em>Immersion Experience Questionnaire — Shortened Form</em> (Cutting et al., 2025),
      followed by custom questions created by the researcher of this study. During the procedure,
      observations of gameplay and informal interviews served as additional data collection.
    </p>
    <p>
      The procedure was repeated among <strong>35 participants</strong>, divided into three
      conditions — diegetic markers, non-diegetic markers, and a control group.
      Narrative immersion was measured with two questionnaires —
      <em>IEQ-SF</em> and <em>NES</em> — and custom questions provided insight specific to
      the study's research question. Analysis of the quantitative data in SPSS pointed
      towards the null hypothesis, meaning <strong>there is no statistical influence
      across conditions on players' narrative immersion</strong>. However, the rest of the
      data points towards a more nuanced conclusion.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- STUDY AT A GLANCE -->
  <section class="research-section">
    <h2>The study at a glance</h2>
<div class="study-stats">
      <div class="stat">
        <span class="stat-number">35</span>
        <span class="stat-label">Participants</span>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <span class="stat-number">3</span>
        <span class="stat-label">Conditions</span>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <span class="stat-number">10<span class="stat-unit">min</span></span>
        <span class="stat-label">of VR play</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>
      Participants were randomly assigned to one of three marker conditions in
      <em>Echoes of Abandonment</em>, a VR experience about a child coping with their parents'
      divorce: <strong>diegetic markers</strong> (a brightness shader),
      <strong>non-diegetic markers</strong> (an exclamation mark icon), and a
      <strong>control group</strong> with no markers. Narrative immersion was assessed
      using the IEQ-SF and NES alongside custom Likert-scale items.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- FINDINGS -->
  <section class="research-section">
    <h2>What I found</h2>

    <div class="finding-card finding-null">
      <div class="finding-tag">Primary finding</div>
      <h3>Marker design did not significantly affect narrative immersion.</h3>
      <p>
        ANOVA and Kruskal-Wallis tests revealed no statistically significant differences
        between conditions, with effect sizes consistently below η² = .02.
        Both hypotheses — that diegetic markers would support immersion, and that
        non-diegetic markers would disturb it — were rejected.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="finding-card finding-surprise">
      <div class="finding-tag">Qualitative insight</div>
      <h3>Non-diegetic markers were perceived as clearer signifiers.</h3>
      <p>
        Open-ended questionnaire responses suggested participants in the non-diegetic
        condition tended to find the exclamation mark markers easier to interpret.
        Diegetic markers received more mixed responses — some appreciated their subtlety
        and immersion-preserving qualities, while others, particularly those with less
        gaming experience, found them insufficiently clear as signifiers.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="finding-card finding-surprise">
      <div class="finding-tag">Qualitative insight</div>
      <h3>Sound design may have acted as a confounding variable.</h3>
      <p>
        The audio design of <em>Echoes of Abandonment</em> appeared to create frustration
        across all conditions, potentially dampening narrative immersion scores uniformly.
        This suggests <strong>auditory UI deserves the same scrutiny as visual UI</strong>
        in VR research.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="finding-card finding-theory">
      <div class="finding-tag">Theoretical implication</div>
      <h3>Marker design may matter less when narrative and UI operate on separate sensory channels.</h3>
      <p>
        In <em>Echoes of Abandonment</em>, the story unfolds auditorily while the markers
        are visual. This suggests the relationship between UI design and narrative
        immersion may be mediated by whether they share the same sensory modality —
        an interpretation that extends Köhle et al. (2021) from visually-driven VR shooters
        to narrative VR experiences.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- DOWNLOADS -->
  <section class="research-section downloads">
    <h2>Read the paper</h2>
    <p>The thesis is available in multiple formats depending on how deep you want to go.</p>
    <div class="downloads-grid">
      <a href="/thesis/paulina-cywoniuk-thesis-draft.pdf" class="download-card download-card-primary" download>
        <span class="download-format">Full thesis</span>
        <span class="download-desc">Complete research with methodology, results, and discussion.</span>
        <span class="download-status">Download PDF →</span>
      </a>
      <div class="download-card download-card-disabled">
        <span class="download-format">1-pager summary</span>
        <span class="download-desc">The TL;DR — for sharing.</span>
        <span class="download-status">Coming soon</span>
      </div>
      <div class="download-card download-card-disabled">
        <span class="download-format">Extended abstract</span>
        <span class="download-desc">8 pages, for conference submission.</span>
        <span class="download-status">Coming soon</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="download-note">
      <em>This is a working draft submitted as part of the Master's of Game Technology
      programme at Breda University of Applied Sciences (2026). The version shared here
      may differ from the final examined version.</em>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- TIPS PREVIEW (acknowledged as coming soon, not broken link) -->
  <section class="research-section tips-preview">
    <h2>Design tips for VR developers</h2>
    <p>
      A planned next step from this research: a library of evidence-based design tips
      for VR developers working with diegetic and non-diegetic UI in narrative experiences.
    </p>
    <p class="tips-coming-soon">
      Currently in development — check back, or reach out via the
      <a href="/contact">contact page</a> to be notified.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- BACK NAV -->
  <section class="research-section back-nav">
    <a href="/" class="back-link">← Back to home</a>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* Shared section spacing */
  .research-hero,
  .research-section {
    max-width: 800px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 2.5rem var(--space-page);
  }

  /* HERO */
  .research-hero {
    padding-top: clamp(3rem, 8vh, 5rem);
  }

  .research-eyebrow {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--color-blue);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

    .research-title {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.5rem);   /* UNCHANGED — same size as always */
    line-height: 1.1;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
margin-left: -2.5rem;
/* align-items: center; */
    /* Wider column than the body text.
       The hero is 800px; this lets the title go wider.
       KNOB: raise 1050px for wider (fewer lines),
             lower for narrower (more lines). */
    width: 810px;
    max-width: 50vw;    /* safety: never wider than 90% of screen (mobile) */
  }

  .research-title em {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--color-purple);
  }

  .research-lede {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
    max-width: 55ch;
  }

  .research-lede .hl {
    background-color: var(--color-cyan);
    color: var(--color-text);
    padding: 0 0.2em;
    border-radius: 4px;
  }

  .research-meta {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
  }

  /* SECTIONS */
  .research-section h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 4vw, 2.5rem);
    color: var(--color-purple);
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .research-section p {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }

  .research-section em {
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .research-section strong {
    color: var(--color-text);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  /* ABSTRACT block — distinct visual treatment */
  .abstract-block {
    /* background-color: var(--color-cyan); */
    outline: 2px solid  var(--color-cyan);
    border-radius: 16px;
    padding: 2.5rem !important;
    max-width: 800px;
  }

  .abstract-block h2 {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    color: var(--color-purple);
  }

  /* STUDY STATS */
  .study-stats {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(140px, 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
    margin: 2rem 0;
    padding: 1.5rem;
    /* background-color: var(--color-orange); */
    border-radius: 12px;
  }

  .stat { text-align: center; }

 .stat-number {
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 3.5rem;
    font-weight: 900;
    color: var(--color-purple);
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }

  .stat-unit {
    /* Override the parent's big font-size — must be explicit */
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    margin-left: 0.1em;
    color: var(--color-purple);
    /* Lift slightly so 'min' aligns with the cap height of '10' */
    vertical-align: 0.6em;
    letter-spacing: 0;
  }

  .stat-label {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin-top: 0.25rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
  }

  /* FINDING CARDS */
  .finding-card {
    padding: 1.75rem 2rem;
    border-radius: 12px;
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }

  .finding-null {
    /* background-color: rgba(243, 146, 55, 0.18); */
    border-left: 4px solid var(--color-orange);
  }

  .finding-surprise {
    /* background-color: rgba(199, 239, 0, 0.2); */
    border-left: 4px solid var(--color-lime);
  }

  .finding-theory {
    /* background-color: rgba(128, 35, 146, 0.08); */
    border-left: 4px solid var(--color-blue);
  }

  .finding-tag {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .finding-card h3 {
    font-size: 1.35rem;
    color: var(--color-purple);
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .finding-card p {
    font-size: 1rem;
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  /* DOWNLOADS */
  .downloads-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(220px, 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .download-card {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    padding: 1.5rem;
    background-color: var(--color-bg);
    border: 1.5px solid var(--color-text);
    border-radius: 12px;
    text-decoration: none;
    color: var(--color-text);
    transition: transform 0.15s ease, background-color 0.2s ease;
  }

  .download-card:hover {
    transform: translateY(-4px);
    background-color: var(--color-lime);
  }

  .download-card-primary {
    background-color: var(--color-purple);
    color: white;
    border-color: var(--color-purple);
  }

  .download-card-primary:hover {
    background-color: var(--color-blue);
    border-color: var(--color-blue);
    color: white;
  }

  .download-card-disabled {
    cursor: default;
    opacity: 0.6;
  }

  .download-card-disabled:hover {
    transform: none;
    background-color: var(--color-bg);
  }

  .download-format {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
  }

  .download-desc {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    opacity: 0.85;
  }

  .download-status {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    opacity: 0.7;
    margin-top: auto;
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .download-note {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    font-size: 0.9rem !important;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
  }

  /* TIPS PREVIEW */
  .tips-preview {
    /* background-color: var(--color-cyan); */
    outline: 2px solid  var(--color-cyan);
    border-radius: 16px;
    padding: 3rem 2rem !important;
  }

  .tips-coming-soon {
    font-size: 0.95rem !important;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .tips-coming-soon a {
    color: var(--color-purple);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-purple);
  }

  /* BACK NAV */
  .back-nav {
    padding-top: 1rem !important;
    padding-bottom: 4rem !important;
  }

  .back-link {
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .back-link:hover {
    color: var(--color-purple);
  }
</style>